                <m>(0,10)</m>
              </point>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="A staircase path made of ten unit steps climbing from the origin to the point ten comma ten, each step going one unit to the right and then one unit up. From ten comma ten the path returns straight left along the line y equals ten to the point zero comma ten. Arrowheads show the direction of travel: rightward on the staircase and leftward on the top segment.">
                <annotation ref="hst-path" text="The staircase path from the origin to ten comma ten and then back along y equals ten to zero comma ten."/>
                <annotation ref="hst-a1" text="An arrowhead on the staircase pointing to the right, the direction of travel."/>
                <annotation ref="hst-a2" text="An arrowhead on the top segment pointing to the left, the direction of travel back to zero comma ten."/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw8-circle">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Calculate the line integral
      </p>

      <me>
      \oint_C \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx
      + y\left[xy + \ln\left(x + \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\right)\right] dy ,
      </me>

      <p>
      where <m>C</m> is the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, with
      counterclockwise orientation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw9-solutions">
      <p>
      Differentiating, <m>\frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
      = \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m> and
      <m>\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} = y\left(y +
      \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\right)</m>, so their difference is
      <m>y^2</m>. By Green's theorem,
      <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>,
      </p>

      <me>
      \oint_C = \iint_R y^2\,dx\,dy
      = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1 r^3\sin^2\theta\,dr\,d\theta
      = \frac{\pi}{4} .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw8-flux">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Calculate the outward flux of the vector field
      <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle x+y,\ -xy \right\rangle</m>
      across the unit circle.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw9-solutions">
      <p>
      Here <m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf{F} = 1 - x</m>, so by the flux form
      of Green's theorem, <xref ref="eq-green-normal"/>,
      </p>

      <me>
      \oint_C \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,ds
      = \iint_R (1-x)\,dA
      = \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{2\pi}\left(1-r\cos\theta\right)
      r\,d\theta\,dr = \pi ,
      </me>

      <p>
      since the <m>x</m>-term integrates to zero by symmetry and what is
      left is the area of the disk.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw8-rose">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Consider the line integral
      <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \left(y^4 - 2y\right) dx
      - \left(6x - 4xy^3\right) dy</m>, where the curve <m>C</m> is
      <m>r = \sin(2\theta)</m>, <m>0 \le \theta \le \frac{\pi}{2}</m>,
      oriented counterclockwise. Note that <m>(r,\theta)</m> are polar
      coordinates.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) Use Green's theorem to calculate the line integral.
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) In physics, this line integral can be interpreted in two
      different ways. Briefly explain.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw9-solutions">
      <p>
      With <m>M = y^4-2y</m> and <m>N = -(6x-4xy^3)</m>,
      <m>\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} = -6+4y^3</m> and
      <m>\frac{\partial M}{\partial y} = 4y^3-2</m>, so their
      difference is <m>-4</m>. The petal has area
      </p>

      <me>
      \int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{\sin 2\theta} r\,dr\,d\theta
      = \int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{\sin^2(2\theta)}{2}\,d\theta
      = \frac{\pi}{8} ,
      </me>

      <p>
      so the integral is <m>(-4)\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)
      = -\frac{\pi}{2}</m>. It is both the circulation of
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around the petal and, read the other way, the
      work done by <m>\mathbf{F}</m> on a particle going once around it.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>
</section>
